arguments.explanation:
$\mathbf{v}_2 = 2\mathbf{v}_1$, so it contributes nothing new — but $\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\mathbf{v}_3$ are not multiples of each other, and two non-parallel vectors in the plane reach every point:
$$
a\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} + c\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}
$$
is solvable for any $(x,y)$ (the coefficient matrix has determinant $1 - 6 = -5 \neq 0$). The third vector is redundant; the first two independent ones already reach the whole plane. Note that a span is made of *sums*, so it is never just a union of lines.", "options": [{"label": "Every vector in $\mathbb{R}^2$ — the whole plane", "value": "whole"}, {"label": "The union of the two lines through the origin along $(1,2)$ and along $(3,1)$", "value": "union"}, {"label": "Just the line through the origin along $(1,2)$, since $\mathbf{v}_2$ is a multiple of $\mathbf{v}_1$", "value": "line"}], "question": "In $\mathbb{R}^2$, take $\mathbf{v}_1 = (1,2)$, $\mathbf{v}_2 = (2,4)$, $\mathbf{v}_3 = (3,1)$.

What is the set of all linear combinations $a\mathbf{v}_1 + b\mathbf{v}_2 + c\mathbf{v}_3$ (all real $a,b,c$)?"}
